body:
Given that this is all functionality to help in the editor, I decided to move all the code back to the `EditorInspectorPluginControl`. This keeps the `Control` class itself free from editor tool bloat.

---

> What theme is the variation supposed to be created in? It works well when the Theme is directly assigned to the Control, but what if it's in a parent Control? Or the Control uses the project's default theme? Or there is no theme at all? Right now the button is always visible and active, even if accepting the variation name does nothing in the end.

Currently, it uses `Control::get_theme_owner_node()` to find the theme it should modify:
```cpp
Node *theme_owner_node = current_control->get_theme_owner_node();
ERR_FAIL_NULL_MSG(theme_owner_node, "No ancestor of this Control has a Theme.");

Control *theme_owner_control = Object::cast_to<Control>(theme_owner_node);
ERR_FAIL_NULL_MSG(theme_owner_control, "The theme owner is not a Control.");

Ref<Theme> theme = theme_owner_control->get_theme();
ERR_FAIL_COND_MSG(theme.is_null() || !theme.is_valid(), "The Theme does not exist or is invalid.");

StringName variation_name;

EditorUndoRedoManager *ur = EditorUndoRedoManager::get_singleton();

ur->create_action("Create Variation from Overrides", UndoRedo::MERGE_DISABLE, *theme);

if (p_name != "") {
	ur->add_do_method(*theme, "set_type_variation", p_name, get_class_name());
	ur->add_undo_method(*theme, "clear_type_variation", p_name);

	ur->force_fixed_history();
	ur->add_do_method(current_control, "set_theme_type_variation", p_name);

	ur->force_fixed_history();
	ur->add_undo_method(current_control, "set_theme_type_variation", "");

	variation_name = p_name;
} else {
	variation_name = current_control->get_theme_type_variation();
}
```
Looking at the code, I think I do see a potential issue: **the Control's theme type variation name may not belong to the theme it finds, but instead a farther ancestor, but right now it assumes that the variation is in the nearest theme**.

If the project's default theme is used, I think the *expected behavior* would be to have these changes apply to that theme. I'm not familiar with project default themes though so it's something I'll have to look into more.

If there's no theme at all, I'm guessing one of the null checks would fail and we'd get an error message. Ideally, it should probably just disable the button until there's a theme for it to push overrides to.


> The editor should display a confirmation dialog before overriding values.

Makes sense to me!

> You can call notify_property_list_changed() on variation change.

Awesome, thanks for the tip! I'll see if I can get that implemented soon.
